===============================================================SINGNED AND UNSIGNED
-- SIGNED
Means the numeric column can store both positive and negative values.
Default behavior if you don’t specify UNSIGNED.
Example:
CREATE TABLE accounts (
    balance INT SIGNED
);
INSERT INTO accounts VALUES (-500);   -- valid
INSERT INTO accounts VALUES (1000);   -- valid

-- UNSIGNED
Means the numeric column can store only non-negative values (0 and positive).
Extends the positive range because negatives are not allowed.
Example:
CREATE TABLE inventory (
    quantity INT UNSIGNED
);
INSERT INTO inventory VALUES (50);    -- valid
INSERT INTO inventory VALUES (0);     -- valid
INSERT INTO inventory VALUES (-10);   -- ERROR (negative not allowed)

-- Why use UNSIGNED?
When you know values will never be negative (like IDs, counts, quantities, prices).
It doubles the positive range compared to SIGNED.



