=====================================================================KEYS
Keys are special columns (or sets of columns) used to uniquely identify rows
and define relationships between tables. They ensure data integrity and prevent duplicates.

-- Types of Keys
1. PRIMARY KEY (main identifier for rows)
   → Uniquely identifies each row in a table.
   → No NULLs, only one per table.

2. FOREIGN KEY (enforces relationships)
   → References the PRIMARY KEY of another table.
   → Enforces relationships between tables.
 
3. UNIQUE KEY (prevents duplication)
   → Ensures all values in a column are unique.
   → Allows NULLs (depends on DBMS).

4. CANDIDATE KEY (potential primary key)
   → Any column(s) that can uniquely identify a row.
   → One becomes the PRIMARY KEY.

5. ALTERNATE KEY (backup identifier)
   → Candidate keys not chosen as PRIMARY KEY.
   → Still uniquely identify rows.

6. SUPER KEY (any unique column set)
   → Any set of columns that can uniquely identify a row.
   → May include extra attributes.

7. COMPOSITE KEY (combines multiple columns)
   → Combination of two or more columns to uniquely identify a row.
   → Used when no single column is unique enough.



===========================================================================PRIMARY & FORIEGN KEY
1. Imagine you have a student table with student IDs.
2. You also have a library table that records which student borrowed which book.
3. The library table needs to know which student each record belongs to.
4. Instead of writing the student name every time, we just store their student ID.

-- Create Students table
CREATE TABLE Students (
    student_id INT PRIMARY KEY,
    name VARCHAR(50)
);
-- Create Library table
CREATE TABLE Library (
    borrowed_id INT PRIMARY KEY,
    book_title VARCHAR(100),
    student_id INT,
    FOREIGN KEY (student_id) REFERENCES Students(student_id)->//this student_id in library must matches the student_id in students
);             //studend_id this name could be anything 

-- What Foreign Key does
1. Ensure validity → you can’t insert a library record with student_id = 99 if no student with ID 99 exists.
2. Maintain relationships → every borrowed book is tied to a real student.
3. Prevent orphan data → no meaningless records floating around without a matching student.

-- Extra Points (good practice)
✔ PRIMARY KEY cannot be NULL.
✔ Only one PRIMARY KEY per table.
✔ FOREIGN KEY can be multiple in a table.
✔ Always define PRIMARY KEY first, then FOREIGN KEY for relationships.
✔ PRIMARY KEY = "original identity card" in the main table.
✔ FOREIGN KEY = "reference copy" in another table pointing back to that identity.



======================================================================CONSTRAINTS
SQL CONSTAINTS ARE USED TO SPECIFY RULES FOR DATA IN A TABLE

-- 1. NOT NULL
ENSURES A COLUMN CANNOT HAVE NULL VALUES
CREATE TABLE Students (
    student_id INT NOT NULL,
    name VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL
);

-- 2. UNIQUE
GUARANTEE ALL VALUES IN A COLUMN ARE DISTINCT
CREATE TABLE Students (
    email VARCHAR(100) UNIQUE
);
-- No two students can share the same email.

-- 3. PRIMARY KEY
COMBINATION OF NOT NULL AND UNIQUE 
IDENTIFIES ROW UNIQUELY
ONLY ONE PRIMARY KEY PER TABLE
CREATE TABLE Students (
    student_id INT PRIMARY KEY,
    name VARCHAR(50)
);
-- Unique + Not Null, main identifier.

-- 4. FOREIGN KEY
LINKS ONE TABLE TO ANOTHER
ENFORCES REFERENTIAL INTEGRITY (CHILD ROWS MUST MATCH PARENT ROWS)
CREATE TABLE Library (
    borrowed_id INT PRIMARY KEY,
    book_title VARCHAR(100),
    student_id INT,
    FOREIGN KEY (student_id) REFERENCES Students(student_id)
);
-- Links Library table to Students table.

-- 5. CHECK
ENSURES VALUES MEEET A CONDITION
CREATE TABLE Students (
    age INT CHECK (age >= 18)
);
-- Only students aged 18 or above allowed.

-- 6. DEFAULT
ASSIGN A DEFAULT VALUE IF NONE IS PROVIDED
CREATE TABLE Students (
    status VARCHAR(20) DEFAULT 'Active'
);
-- If no status given, defaults to "Active".

-- 7. AUTO INCREMENT / IDENTITY
AUTOMATICALLY  GENERATES SEQUENTIAL VALUES FOR A COLUMN(USUALLY PRIMARY KEY)
-- MySQL style
CREATE TABLE Students (
    student_id INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
    name VARCHAR(50)
);
-- SQL Server style
CREATE TABLE Students (
    student_id INT IDENTITY(1,1) PRIMARY KEY,
    name VARCHAR(50)
);
-- Automatically generates sequential IDs.



